## Proof

Let $U \in \mathbb{C}^{n \times n}$ be unitary. We seek a unitary $V$ maximizing $\Re\,\mathrm{tr}(UV)$.

**Step 1: Reduction to maximizing $\Re\,\mathrm{tr}(W)$ over unitary $W$.**

Since $U$ is unitary, the map $V \mapsto W := UV$ is a bijection from the unitary group $U(n)$ onto itself (its inverse is $W \mapsto V = U^{*}W$). Hence

$$\max_{V \in U(n)} \Re\,\mathrm{tr}(UV) \;=\; \max_{W \in U(n)} \Re\,\mathrm{tr}(W).$$

**Step 2: Spectral bound.**

Every unitary $W$ is normal, so it is unitarily diagonalizable: $W = P\,\mathrm{diag}(\lambda_1,\dots,\lambda_n)\,P^{*}$ with $P$ unitary and $|\lambda_i|=1$ for all $i$. Then

$$\mathrm{tr}(W) = \sum_{i=1}^{n} \lambda_i, \qquad \Re\,\mathrm{tr}(W) = \sum_{i=1}^{n} \Re(\lambda_i).$$

Since $|\lambda_i|=1$, we have $\Re(\lambda_i) \le |\lambda_i| = 1$, with equality iff $\lambda_i = 1$. Therefore

$$\Re\,\mathrm{tr}(W) = \sum_{i=1}^{n} \Re(\lambda_i) \le \sum_{i=1}^{n} 1 = n.$$

**Step 3: Attainment.**

Equality holds iff every $\lambda_i = 1$, i.e., iff $W = I_n$. Tracing back through the bijection, $W = UV = I_n$ gives

$$V = U^{-1} = U^{*}.$$

This $V = U^{*}$ is itself unitary (conjugate transpose of a unitary matrix), so it is feasible, and it achieves $\Re\,\mathrm{tr}(UV) = \Re\,\mathrm{tr}(I_n) = n$.

**Conclusion.**

The unique maximizer is

$$\boxed{V = U^{*}}$$

and the maximum value is $\Re\,\mathrm{tr}(UV) = n$, the dimension of the matrix. Uniqueness follows because equality in Step 2 forces all eigenvalues of $W=UV$ to be $1$, hence $W=I_n$, hence $V=U^{*}$.

